00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:33.540
You see a lot of thoughts
here in the chat about it too.

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:36.990
I think Marlene, you
weighed in first about this.

00:13:36.990 --> 00:13:37.830
You want to jump in?

00:13:40.794 --> 00:13:45.370
[LAUGHS] So everybody asked
me because I'm the one data

00:13:45.370 --> 00:13:48.400
scientist in our family of
companies, what's the deal,

00:13:48.400 --> 00:13:49.840
yow, with ChatGPT?

00:13:49.840 --> 00:13:53.380
And overall, I try
to say, it's a tool.

00:13:53.380 --> 00:13:56.680
With a pencil, you can
draw, you can write prose,

00:13:56.680 --> 00:13:57.820
you could stab somebody.

00:13:57.820 --> 00:14:01.000
So I keep telling them, it's
just like any other tool,

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:02.380
depends on how it's wielded.

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:05.830
But so far, the biggest
nonsupporters of it

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:08.050
has been the
communications department.

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:10.960
Anybody in journalism is
very threatened by it,

00:14:10.960 --> 00:14:14.080
but I keep trying to tell them,
it's great for brainstorming.

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:18.670
Don't think of it as
like polished copy right.

00:14:18.670 --> 00:14:19.450
So it's funny.

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:21.035
I think you're going
to end up being--

00:14:21.035 --> 00:14:22.660
each one of you,
you're going to end up

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:24.077
having a lot of
people come to you

00:14:24.077 --> 00:14:28.450
and trying to be
seen as an expert--

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:31.720
a domain expert in it.

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:33.430
My advice to you
especially, try

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:35.290
to be objective
even though you have

00:14:35.290 --> 00:14:37.630
your own opinions about it.

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:41.210
It's important for us to be
good stewards of knowledge.

00:14:41.210 --> 00:14:45.160
So I do my best to tell
people, envision a lot,

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:48.340
be cautious, but also
feel free to dream

00:14:48.340 --> 00:14:49.790
because it's not going away.

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:52.480
So that's been my
message to anybody

00:14:52.480 --> 00:14:54.490
who's asking my
company, which has

00:14:54.490 --> 00:14:56.330
been a lot in the
last couple of weeks.

00:14:56.330 --> 00:14:57.040
That's for sure.

00:14:57.040 --> 00:14:59.140
So that's my $0.02.

00:14:59.140 --> 00:15:01.990
My message is
always talk to legal.

00:15:01.990 --> 00:15:03.430
[LAUGHTER]

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:06.167
I know there are a lot
of concerns about--

00:15:06.167 --> 00:15:08.500
not only, obviously, sending
your data to their servers,

00:15:08.500 --> 00:15:11.650
but even if you have a local
instance of a model running.

00:15:11.650 --> 00:15:13.908
There's always concern
about bias in the model

00:15:13.908 --> 00:15:16.450
and there's always concern about
some of the copyright stuff.

00:15:16.450 --> 00:15:19.910
So if you're producing
code and then using it,

00:15:19.910 --> 00:15:21.160
where did that code come from?

00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:23.680
That's all obfuscated
behind the model.

00:15:23.680 --> 00:15:27.070
So definitely, I'm so
excited about the tech.

00:15:27.070 --> 00:15:29.440
I know people in my
company are, but gosh,

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:31.654
everyone has to be so careful.

00:15:31.654 --> 00:15:34.120
This was a problem before.

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:34.760
Right.

00:15:34.760 --> 00:15:37.270
Learn from Samsung
who's shared a bunch

00:15:37.270 --> 00:15:38.940
of sensitive
information with a model

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:40.690
and now it's all out
in the public domain.

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:41.770
So be careful.

00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:43.060
Yeah.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:47.140
I see Andy, you put
a thought about how

00:15:47.140 --> 00:15:49.742
you might be using ChatGPT.

00:15:49.742 --> 00:15:50.950
Do you want to jump in there?

00:15:54.350 --> 00:15:55.310
Yeah, sure.

00:15:55.310 --> 00:15:57.840
So I work for Teach for
America as a data scientist.

00:15:57.840 --> 00:16:01.100
So we have 10,000 applications
coming every year,

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:04.145
and we do a first pass
with our NLP models.

00:16:04.145 --> 00:16:05.520
But we've had this
huge challenge

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:09.650
now because we are the target
audience for the early adopters

00:16:09.650 --> 00:16:11.750
of GPT, which is
college aged students

00:16:11.750 --> 00:16:13.520
who are writing essays.

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:17.480
And because we basically have
trained college age students

00:16:17.480 --> 00:16:20.900
to write the exact same essay
that GPT wants to write,

00:16:20.900 --> 00:16:23.540
we're now dealing with this
huge repercussion of all

00:16:23.540 --> 00:16:25.990
of our historical
training data sets

00:16:25.990 --> 00:16:31.280
for NLP passing are
trained prior to GPT,

00:16:31.280 --> 00:16:32.840
and now we have
this huge influx.

00:16:32.840 --> 00:16:35.970
So it's really become
a challenge not on--

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:39.980
we also use GPT
internally, but it's

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:43.130
more on the receiving end of
seeing the products of GPT

00:16:43.130 --> 00:16:46.260
has been a huge challenge.

00:16:46.260 --> 00:16:47.770
Thank you.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:51.155
I see Mike, you had
a question earlier.

00:16:51.155 --> 00:16:52.530
It's funny to be
like, hey, Mike.

00:16:52.530 --> 00:16:56.117
You had a question for
me, can you ask it?

00:16:56.117 --> 00:16:56.700
Hey, Rachel?

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:58.908
How would you like to be
[INAUDIBLE] that I asked you

00:16:58.908 --> 00:17:00.330
in the chat.

00:17:00.330 --> 00:17:01.740
It was basically--

00:17:01.740 --> 00:17:06.310
I know that you have come
from a marketing background.

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:12.773
And so oftentimes, I've said
that data scientists are geeks.

00:17:12.773 --> 00:17:14.190
And so if we're
selling to people,

00:17:14.190 --> 00:17:15.720
we're selling to other geeks.

00:17:15.720 --> 00:17:18.480
And we're trying to hook them in
with how clever the solution is

00:17:18.480 --> 00:17:26.680
or how it's going to help
us do geeky stuff better.

00:17:26.680 --> 00:17:29.860
Knowing about
marketing, what skills

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:32.440
would you recommend
data scientists

00:17:32.440 --> 00:17:36.400
to look into for selling
upwards to managers and people

00:17:36.400 --> 00:17:38.880
who aren't the geeks?

00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:40.740
Yeah.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:44.280
So something I think
that is really important

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:49.380
is actually taking the time
to make something look pretty,

00:17:49.380 --> 00:17:52.350
and there's a lot
of tools out there

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:53.700
that you can use to do that.

00:17:53.700 --> 00:17:58.740
One that I always use
for like groups as Canva

00:17:58.740 --> 00:18:02.520
because there's a bunch of
different design templates.

00:18:02.520 --> 00:18:05.640
But I always find if you
put a little bit of effort

00:18:05.640 --> 00:18:11.490
into what something looks like,
people take it more seriously.

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:17.980
And so I was thinking about that
with showing an idea for an app

00:18:17.980 --> 00:18:25.060
to your leadership team trying
to actually use the colors that

00:18:25.060 --> 00:18:27.700
match your brand or
putting your logo in it

00:18:27.700 --> 00:18:31.630
so that people immediately see
it as something like oh, this

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:33.890
is how we would use
this at our company.

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:36.130
Even if it's completely
made up data,

00:18:36.130 --> 00:18:41.070
I think having the
brand guidelines there,

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:43.990
it just changes the
way people view it.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:50.380
That's one thing I've
been thinking about.

00:18:50.380 --> 00:18:53.890
But I mean, I still
struggle with this too,

00:18:53.890 --> 00:18:55.100
pushing ideas forward.

00:18:55.100 --> 00:18:59.200
I literally just got off a call
about something a little bit--

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:02.210
probably an hour ago.

00:19:02.210 --> 00:19:08.670
And one mistake I made was not
thinking of the sales process

00:19:08.670 --> 00:19:11.590
with my own idea.

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:14.200
If I get really excited
about something,

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:17.260
I want to go make it
happen immediately.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:20.950
And sometimes that's
good, but sometimes you

00:19:20.950 --> 00:19:24.960
need to make sure that you are
actually bringing everybody

00:19:24.960 --> 00:19:31.090
on board to see your vision
and your goal for the project,

00:19:31.090 --> 00:19:34.290
especially in
leadership, so that they

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:35.940
can help push it forward.

00:19:35.940 --> 00:19:38.760
Because I've learned, you're not
always going to be in the room

00:19:38.760 --> 00:19:41.520
when somebody is
discussing your ideas.

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:43.830
So it's important that
they understand it too.

00:19:47.600 --> 00:19:49.220
Thanks.

00:19:49.220 --> 00:19:50.000
Does that help?

00:19:50.000 --> 00:19:56.930
[LAUGHS] I was looking for
a different direction there.

00:19:56.930 --> 00:19:58.220
Well, it's--

00:20:01.580 --> 00:20:04.580
I guess there's several
directions I was thinking.

00:20:04.580 --> 00:20:09.290
One is we have a good
solution or we've

00:20:09.290 --> 00:20:13.610
got a good idea that we want
to get management buy in from,

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:14.750
which is a tricky process.

00:20:14.750 --> 00:20:19.710
And you get a limited
amount of their time.

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:22.850
So how do you ensure that all
the things they want to know

00:20:22.850 --> 00:20:24.090
are ticked?

00:20:24.090 --> 00:20:24.590
Yeah.

00:20:24.590 --> 00:20:26.510
There's another
question, which is possibly

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:29.390
for a different
day, but it's more

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:31.610
you're trying to sell
to your peers, so

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:33.080
to other data scientists.

00:20:33.080 --> 00:20:35.480
Hey, we've got a process
well defined here.

00:20:35.480 --> 00:20:38.300
How many times do--
and in how many ways

00:20:38.300 --> 00:20:40.820
do you keep telling
them that same thing

00:20:40.820 --> 00:20:41.820
so that the thing drops?

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:45.700
Yeah.

00:20:45.700 --> 00:20:47.920
I mean, a piece of advice
I can share here just

00:20:47.920 --> 00:20:53.690
from community building and
also getting our team on board

00:20:53.690 --> 00:20:58.200
to come to events
as well is just

00:20:58.200 --> 00:21:01.920
having one on one conversations
with people to learn about some

00:21:01.920 --> 00:21:05.760
of the problems that they're
facing every day in their work,

00:21:05.760 --> 00:21:10.170
and then trying to incorporate
that into whatever you

00:21:10.170 --> 00:21:12.400
are proposing.

00:21:12.400 --> 00:21:16.590
So that you can build them
up as your own champions

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:19.695
to help you pushing
this forward.

00:21:22.620 --> 00:21:25.290
That's been really
helpful for me.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.570
I know that it's
trying to compare

00:21:27.570 --> 00:21:29.580
like two different things
here because I'm not

00:21:29.580 --> 00:21:32.130
a data scientist
pushing this forward,

00:21:32.130 --> 00:21:37.688
but maybe even just thinking
about pushing the Data Science

00:21:37.688 --> 00:21:39.480
Hangout forward and
making sure that people

00:21:39.480 --> 00:21:42.015
see the value in that too.

00:21:45.660 --> 00:21:48.150
Anybody else have
thoughts on this

00:21:48.150 --> 00:21:52.020
and selling to the leadership
and sharing your ideas?

00:21:54.930 --> 00:21:57.390
I will say that
getting to know people

00:21:57.390 --> 00:21:59.790
on a more personal
level is really helpful.

00:21:59.790 --> 00:22:02.040
If you can have
conversations with leadership

00:22:02.040 --> 00:22:04.650
that are not about work
and get to know them

00:22:04.650 --> 00:22:07.290
and what matters to
them generally in life,

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:12.160
and even what matters to
them in a business sense.

00:22:12.160 --> 00:22:15.630
Everybody has a different
thing that they're championing

00:22:15.630 --> 00:22:17.890
and that they're really
interested in moving forward.

00:22:17.890 --> 00:22:20.197
And so finding out
what is individually

00:22:20.197 --> 00:22:22.530
motivating to these people
that you're trying to talk to

00:22:22.530 --> 00:22:24.960
can be really helpful because
then you can speak to that

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:28.860
and you're not just blowing
your own horn a bunch.

00:22:28.860 --> 00:22:30.150
You can blow their horn.

00:22:30.150 --> 00:22:34.350
I think that that's really
helpful and important.

00:22:34.350 --> 00:22:35.840
Absolutely.

00:22:35.840 --> 00:22:38.450
And I've been trying to
take some of the advice

00:22:38.450 --> 00:22:40.812
that you all share
here too and all

00:22:40.812 --> 00:22:42.020
the different future leaders.

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:47.740
And I remember, I
think Emmy [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:47.740 --> 00:22:50.980
was sharing about repeating
the things that you really

00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:52.390
care about.

00:22:52.390 --> 00:22:56.080
And not just
thinking because you

00:22:56.080 --> 00:22:58.810
said something or proposed
something as an idea one

00:22:58.810 --> 00:23:02.530
time that it's going
to be accepted.

00:23:02.530 --> 00:23:05.830
You have to keep
talking about it.

00:23:05.830 --> 00:23:08.920
And then I've also been
trying to put my ideas

00:23:08.920 --> 00:23:15.470
in the lens of our company OKRs
or objectives and key results

00:23:15.470 --> 00:23:18.100
so that when they're talking
about those at a leadership

00:23:18.100 --> 00:23:21.580
level, they think
about my project

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:25.645
because it was put into those
exact words or those terms.

00:23:29.680 --> 00:23:31.670
Ahnas, I see you have
your hand raised.

00:23:31.670 --> 00:23:32.170
Yeah.

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:33.670
Just a little--

00:23:33.670 --> 00:23:37.330
I guess in addition to
that piece of advice,

00:23:37.330 --> 00:23:42.310
I would say that when
speaking with leaders,

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:44.390
sometimes saying less is more.

00:23:44.390 --> 00:23:49.750
We tend to overshare the
technical aspects and we're

00:23:49.750 --> 00:23:51.280
very--

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:54.880
and sometimes, we feel the
need to talk into those details

00:23:54.880 --> 00:23:56.680
to make our point.

00:23:56.680 --> 00:24:00.820
But they're not as interested
in the technical pieces.

00:24:00.820 --> 00:24:04.840
Just speak to them at
a normal human level,

00:24:04.840 --> 00:24:06.520
and they trust you as an expert.

00:24:06.520 --> 00:24:08.770
There is that aspect.

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:11.350
You just have to come from
their angle as much as possible.

00:24:11.350 --> 00:24:14.560
But I feel like that's something
I've learned more recently when

00:24:14.560 --> 00:24:16.902
I'm explaining a model or
I'm explaining a solution.

00:24:16.902 --> 00:24:19.360
I feel like I want to go into
some of the technical details

00:24:19.360 --> 00:24:20.710
where it's like that's not--

00:24:20.710 --> 00:24:22.418
they're not too worried
about that piece.

00:24:22.418 --> 00:24:24.190
They're just worried about the--

00:24:24.190 --> 00:24:25.990
how does this impact
them or how is this

00:24:25.990 --> 00:24:30.010
going to impact organization?

00:24:30.010 --> 00:24:31.600
Definitely.

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:35.770
Kaitlin, I see you just put
an example into the chat.

00:24:35.770 --> 00:24:39.040
If you want to weigh in here.

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:39.760
Yeah, sure.

00:24:39.760 --> 00:24:42.910
So I was just saying,
at my old company,

00:24:42.910 --> 00:24:46.690
I did end up pitching data for
a good center of excellence,

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:49.960
and it took a few
pitch decks that I

00:24:49.960 --> 00:24:52.600
made on campus, by the way.

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:55.150
But now I think the
biggest feedback

00:24:55.150 --> 00:24:59.710
I got from that is just
echoing what Ahna said here,

00:24:59.710 --> 00:25:02.470
don't try and be technical
with non-technical people.

00:25:02.470 --> 00:25:04.430
That's truly not
what they care about.

00:25:04.430 --> 00:25:09.010
And I think putting yourself
in the shoes of whoever you're

00:25:09.010 --> 00:25:11.050
pitching to is so important.

00:25:11.050 --> 00:25:15.250
And focusing not on the
outcomes for yourself

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:17.860
or I want to do this
idea because xyz

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:19.390
is my passion, da, da, da.

00:25:19.390 --> 00:25:21.940
They don't really care that
that's your passion so much,

00:25:21.940 --> 00:25:25.607
but they want to know how it's
going to help them actually

00:25:25.607 --> 00:25:28.190
not how it's going to help you
in your career and your future.

00:25:28.190 --> 00:25:30.610
But so yeah, I ended up
doing a couple of pitch decks

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:33.490
definitely, a lot of meetings.

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:35.110
And then I think
something that's

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:37.270
really helpful for
higher UPS to see

00:25:37.270 --> 00:25:42.310
is almost a 30, 60, 90 day
plan on what the outcomes may

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:43.495
be of your idea.

00:25:46.300 --> 00:25:47.300
That's really helpful.

00:25:47.300 --> 00:25:48.830
Thanks, Caitlin.

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:49.990
Yeah.

00:25:49.990 --> 00:25:52.540
I see there was a
question in the chat

00:25:52.540 --> 00:25:58.060
if there's a Slack or Discord
or some group for everybody

00:25:58.060 --> 00:26:00.130
to connect after the
fact with the Hangouts

00:26:00.130 --> 00:26:04.840
or where to find the
Hangout recordings?

00:26:04.840 --> 00:26:06.850
I see Libby mentioned
the YouTube.

00:26:06.850 --> 00:26:10.180
If anybody wants to share
the YouTube playlist where

00:26:10.180 --> 00:26:13.460
all the recordings are,
that would be super helpful.

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:17.470
But right now, we don't
have a great solution

00:26:17.470 --> 00:26:19.270
for the ongoing conversations.

00:26:19.270 --> 00:26:21.970
I would love to have
that in the future.

00:26:21.970 --> 00:26:25.550
Right now, we just
have a LinkedIn group,

00:26:25.550 --> 00:26:27.020
which I'll share here.

00:26:27.020 --> 00:26:30.850
I know that's not the best
format for having conversation

00:26:30.850 --> 00:26:33.040
sometimes, but it's
what we have right now.

00:26:33.040 --> 00:26:36.595
But we can definitely explore
other options there too.

00:26:39.460 --> 00:26:42.980
A Slido question
that was asked is--

00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:45.740
for the whole audience, do you
have any favorite data science

00:26:45.740 --> 00:26:47.660
podcasts or YouTube channels?

00:26:52.780 --> 00:26:54.400
I see a lot being shared.

00:26:54.400 --> 00:26:55.300
Data humans.

00:26:55.300 --> 00:26:56.330
Libby's podcast.

00:26:56.330 --> 00:26:58.036
Absolutely

00:26:58.036 --> 00:27:01.000
Also, me, myself, and AI too
have been pretty interesting.

00:27:01.000 --> 00:27:04.620
They just recently
had a couple from--

00:27:04.620 --> 00:27:05.640
one was an airline.

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:06.510
I think it was--

00:27:06.510 --> 00:27:08.340
I want to say it
was Delta, and then

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.300
another from the LEGO group.

00:27:10.300 --> 00:27:13.620
So as a LEGO nerd,
that was also really

00:27:13.620 --> 00:27:18.430
cool to see data science and AI
and LEGO and things like that.

00:27:18.430 --> 00:27:19.800
That's cool.

00:27:19.800 --> 00:27:22.090
Thank you all for sharing
them in the chat too.

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:23.580
We'll have to go
collect all these.

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:25.170
Mike, I see you have
your hand raised?

00:27:25.170 --> 00:27:25.670
Yeah.

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:29.790
I mean, we can't talk about data
humans with Libby on this call

00:27:29.790 --> 00:27:33.480
without Libby giving us
the elevator pitch of--

00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:35.310
Yes, absolutely.

00:27:35.310 --> 00:27:37.660
Yes.

00:27:37.660 --> 00:27:39.880
I would be happy to, yeah.

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:41.860
did humans doesn't
have a ton of episodes.

00:27:41.860 --> 00:27:43.720
It's something I'm
doing on the side.

00:27:43.720 --> 00:27:46.240
It's like every
month or six weeks

00:27:46.240 --> 00:27:48.190
whenever I can get it
in and convince somebody

00:27:48.190 --> 00:27:50.110
to come on the podcast.

00:27:50.110 --> 00:27:53.050
But I started it
because I realized

00:27:53.050 --> 00:27:56.343
that there was a lack of
vulnerability and realness

00:27:56.343 --> 00:27:58.510
when a lot of people,
especially people on LinkedIn,

00:27:58.510 --> 00:27:59.500
are talking about this science.

00:27:59.500 --> 00:28:00.580
It's like all wins.

00:28:00.580 --> 00:28:02.860
Oh, I'm doing this big
huge modeling thing.

00:28:02.860 --> 00:28:05.122
And when you meet them,
sometimes you realize,

00:28:05.122 --> 00:28:06.830
they aren't actually
doing that for work.

00:28:06.830 --> 00:28:08.770
They're doing that
on the side for fun.

00:28:08.770 --> 00:28:15.010
Not everybody is a full
time R package developer.

00:28:15.010 --> 00:28:17.800
It has the potential
to make us all

00:28:17.800 --> 00:28:20.290
feel like, everybody else
is doing something so much

00:28:20.290 --> 00:28:21.320
cooler than we are.

00:28:21.320 --> 00:28:22.143
They must be.

00:28:22.143 --> 00:28:23.560
Like, they must
be doing something

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:25.935
so much cooler than what I'm
doing because what I'm doing

00:28:25.935 --> 00:28:28.540
is a bunch of Excel still
and my company doesn't

00:28:28.540 --> 00:28:31.510
want to have a code tidyverse
approach to analytics and all

00:28:31.510 --> 00:28:33.450
this stuff.

00:28:33.450 --> 00:28:36.840
And really, we're all pretty
similar on the whole--

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:40.740
there's still a ton of data
people using Excel and using

00:28:40.740 --> 00:28:43.560
SaaS and using things
that are not crazy

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:45.700
huge machine learning models.

00:28:45.700 --> 00:28:50.340
So I just wanted a place to talk
to everyday data people, not

00:28:50.340 --> 00:28:51.720
CEOs.

00:28:51.720 --> 00:28:53.100
Because there's
a ton of podcasts

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:57.480
that are CEOs or super senior
data scientists at Spotify

00:28:57.480 --> 00:28:59.370
or whatever it is,
and that's fantastic.

00:28:59.370 --> 00:29:01.110
Learning from those
people is great.

00:29:01.110 --> 00:29:04.740
But what I'm interested in is
the everyday people like me

00:29:04.740 --> 00:29:09.000
and people who are just
doing normal stuff what

00:29:09.000 --> 00:29:09.730
are they doing.

00:29:09.730 --> 00:29:14.100
We don't get a peek behind that
vulnerable curtain very often.

00:29:14.100 --> 00:29:17.340
And then, of course, Eric
is also on this call.

00:29:17.340 --> 00:29:19.590
Eric was the first podcast
I ever listened to.

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:22.092
When I first started learning
R, I felt really alone.

00:29:22.092 --> 00:29:23.550
There's no way that
anybody else is

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:27.150
this excited about a
programming language as I am.

00:29:27.150 --> 00:29:30.960
I'm like sitting there in my
first R programming class.

00:29:30.960 --> 00:29:32.070
Everybody's really bored.

00:29:32.070 --> 00:29:33.520
I'm super excited.

00:29:33.520 --> 00:29:35.430
I have a copy of the
grammar of graphics.

00:29:35.430 --> 00:29:38.970
I am absolutely just high
invested in this thing,

00:29:38.970 --> 00:29:40.380
and I found Eric's podcast.

00:29:40.380 --> 00:29:43.658
And I was like, there were
other people out there like me.

00:29:43.658 --> 00:29:47.080
[LAUGHS]

00:29:47.080 --> 00:29:47.920
Thank you, Libby.

00:29:47.920 --> 00:29:49.880
And Eric, now we're going
to have to have you give--

00:29:49.880 --> 00:29:50.463
Yes, please.

00:29:50.463 --> 00:29:52.660
--a little overview too.

00:29:52.660 --> 00:29:53.630
It's amazing.

00:29:53.630 --> 00:29:56.260
That's why I started
the whole thing.

00:29:56.260 --> 00:29:59.110
Is I wanted to
connect with people.

00:29:59.110 --> 00:30:04.660
And I cannot think of a better
thing I could have done even

00:30:04.660 --> 00:30:08.140
though I was really running with
no knowledge of how to do any

00:30:08.140 --> 00:30:09.100
of this whatsoever.

00:30:09.100 --> 00:30:10.970
But no.

00:30:10.970 --> 00:30:13.340
Really, it's about
connecting with people,

00:30:13.340 --> 00:30:16.120
but I'm really
passionate about sharing

00:30:16.120 --> 00:30:19.430
what other people are doing that
I'm learning from every day.

00:30:19.430 --> 00:30:22.390
And so that's why the R
Weekly Project, once I

00:30:22.390 --> 00:30:25.120
got involved with that,
I realized, well, yeah,

00:30:25.120 --> 00:30:26.140
we have the website.

00:30:26.140 --> 00:30:27.520
Every week, we have a new issue.

00:30:27.520 --> 00:30:31.730
But let's have another avenue to
deliver this great content out

00:30:31.730 --> 00:30:32.230
there.

00:30:32.230 --> 00:30:35.350
And that's why I just started
our weekly highlights,

00:30:35.350 --> 00:30:38.200
and it's been a
joy to work with.

00:30:38.200 --> 00:30:39.590
I cannot do it alone.

00:30:39.590 --> 00:30:41.140
I say this every week.

00:30:41.140 --> 00:30:43.570
So Mike Thomas, my
co-host here, is

00:30:43.570 --> 00:30:45.070
one of the biggest
motivators for me

00:30:45.070 --> 00:30:47.980
to keep going because it's
a weekly show with a day

00:30:47.980 --> 00:30:48.940
job with kids.

00:30:48.940 --> 00:30:52.600
It is a lot to fit
in, but R is my hobby.

00:30:52.600 --> 00:30:55.370
I'm still-- open
source is my jam.

00:30:55.370 --> 00:30:56.650
I love talking about it.

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:59.880
So I figured, just throw it
out there, see if it resonates.

00:30:59.880 --> 00:31:00.880
And sure enough, it has.

00:31:00.880 --> 00:31:03.220
But yeah, I love
hearing the stories

00:31:03.220 --> 00:31:05.492
and being able to put
the spotlight on others

00:31:05.492 --> 00:31:06.200
in the community.

00:31:06.200 --> 00:31:07.492
They were doing awesome things.

00:31:07.492 --> 00:31:09.100
So it's a true pleasure to do.

00:31:11.870 --> 00:31:15.050
This feels like such
a heartwarming community

00:31:15.050 --> 00:31:16.530
call right now.

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:17.855
And I just wanted to say--

00:31:17.855 --> 00:31:20.060
take an opportunity
to say, thank you

00:31:20.060 --> 00:31:22.820
to everybody here
who is also helped

00:31:22.820 --> 00:31:25.400
make this space what it is.

00:31:25.400 --> 00:31:29.240
And I absolutely love getting
to hang out with you all every

00:31:29.240 --> 00:31:29.940
Thursday.

00:31:29.940 --> 00:31:31.640
And if I think back to it--

00:31:31.640 --> 00:31:35.240
I think we're going on about
two years in a month or so,

00:31:35.240 --> 00:31:37.980
which is amazing.

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:40.010
But if I think back
to two years ago

00:31:40.010 --> 00:31:42.770
and I thought we didn't
have a featured leader

00:31:42.770 --> 00:31:44.630
and we were just going
to join in all chat,

00:31:44.630 --> 00:31:47.090
I would be freaking out.

00:31:47.090 --> 00:31:50.240
And I just love that
we're all able to have

00:31:50.240 --> 00:31:52.110
this open conversation here.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:55.250
So I just want to say thank
you to all of you here too.

00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:56.655
Thank you, Rachel.

00:31:56.655 --> 00:31:58.280
Everybody, round of
applause to Rachel.

00:31:58.280 --> 00:31:59.010
[LAUGHS]

00:31:59.010 --> 00:32:00.260
You're our fearless leader--

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:00.760
Thank you.

00:32:00.760 --> 00:32:02.570
--whether you know it or not.

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:04.280
Thank you.

00:32:04.280 --> 00:32:07.460
Rory, I see you had some
thoughts on something

00:32:07.460 --> 00:32:10.610
we were talking about a little
bit earlier on the topic

00:32:10.610 --> 00:32:11.690
of spreading ideas.

00:32:11.690 --> 00:32:12.890
Do you want to jump in here?

00:32:15.932 --> 00:32:16.890
Can you guys hear me?

00:32:16.890 --> 00:32:19.860
I just hadn't had
audio or video one.

00:32:19.860 --> 00:32:21.210
Yes, I can.

00:32:21.210 --> 00:32:22.140
Yeah.

00:32:22.140 --> 00:32:23.260
Just a little background.

00:32:23.260 --> 00:32:26.700
So I work here as a
behavioral scientist,

00:32:26.700 --> 00:32:30.030
and my big interest is in
organizational network analysis

00:32:30.030 --> 00:32:34.390
and a lot of relationship
dynamic in organizations.

00:32:34.390 --> 00:32:36.390
And I think one of the
things that doesn't often

00:32:36.390 --> 00:32:40.980
get talked about is how the
relationships that you have

00:32:40.980 --> 00:32:43.710
are really important with
influencing people and getting

00:32:43.710 --> 00:32:45.510
buy in.

00:32:45.510 --> 00:32:49.980
And one of my favorite
resources or people to follow

00:32:49.980 --> 00:32:53.370
is this guy named Mike Arena
who used to be at a leading

00:32:53.370 --> 00:32:56.400
their talent organization.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:33:00.270
And he has recently done a
lot of research on remote work

00:33:00.270 --> 00:33:04.230
on hybrid work on
relationships and innovation.

00:33:04.230 --> 00:33:06.690
And he's generally found
for a lot of that research

00:33:06.690 --> 00:33:11.560
that it's best to spend a lot
of time incubating your ideas.

00:33:11.560 --> 00:33:14.088
If you come up with an idea,
you're passionate about it,

00:33:14.088 --> 00:33:15.630
like were talking
about, Rachel, it's

00:33:15.630 --> 00:33:18.400
great to socialize
that with a few people,

00:33:18.400 --> 00:33:20.913
especially if they're
at your level.

00:33:20.913 --> 00:33:22.830
And maybe they can help
you improve that idea.

00:33:22.830 --> 00:33:24.690
And once you really
build that out, then it

00:33:24.690 --> 00:33:26.970
becomes a good
opportunity to scale

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:29.790
and you have a network
of support around that

00:33:29.790 --> 00:33:32.670
can help you build that up.

00:33:32.670 --> 00:33:34.417
I've seen that play out a lot.

00:33:34.417 --> 00:33:36.750
I don't know if anyone else
has had a similar experience

00:33:36.750 --> 00:33:38.292
in their organization
where they felt

00:33:38.292 --> 00:33:41.130
once they had a
good coalition, they

00:33:41.130 --> 00:33:42.670
were able to get
somebody buy in.

00:33:42.670 --> 00:33:45.630
But I feel like we don't always
talk about the relationships

00:33:45.630 --> 00:33:47.735
we have either at work
or in our personal lives

00:33:47.735 --> 00:33:49.110
and how much of
a difference that

00:33:49.110 --> 00:33:52.375
makes in influencing people
to do one thing or another.

00:33:52.375 --> 00:33:54.000
So I just want to
call that out in case

00:33:54.000 --> 00:33:55.560
that was helpful to
people as they're

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:58.940
trying to pitch new ideas.

00:33:58.940 --> 00:33:59.660
Absolutely.

00:33:59.660 --> 00:34:00.800
Thank you, Rory.

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:02.450
Yeah, no problem.

00:34:02.450 --> 00:34:05.945
Anybody else have ideas
on that topic to share too?

00:34:08.554 --> 00:34:09.054
Yeah.

00:34:09.054 --> 00:34:09.920
I'll just reinforce.

00:34:09.920 --> 00:34:13.350
We did the same thing when
we started our internal user

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:13.850
group.

00:34:13.850 --> 00:34:15.800
It was started as
just an R user group,

00:34:15.800 --> 00:34:19.219
and it was basically
the same five of us

00:34:19.219 --> 00:34:22.850
that were interested and just
presenting back and forth

00:34:22.850 --> 00:34:26.570
to each other each
month with very little

00:34:26.570 --> 00:34:29.270
variety in the presenters.

00:34:29.270 --> 00:34:31.310
But eventually, we
started to expand it,

00:34:31.310 --> 00:34:35.300
started to include
other source languages.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:37.520
And now, we've literally
got hundreds of users

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:41.449
across of the company
pitching new ideas,

00:34:41.449 --> 00:34:44.000
sharing things that they've
done, and just really cool

00:34:44.000 --> 00:34:46.639
to have every stage too.

00:34:46.639 --> 00:34:48.980
We've got folks
who are brand new

00:34:48.980 --> 00:34:53.179
and have heard of R, Python,
or something and want to learn.

00:34:53.179 --> 00:34:55.790
We've also got people who are
super advanced and teaching

00:34:55.790 --> 00:34:57.830
very complex topics.

00:34:57.830 --> 00:35:03.660
So that snowball effect
has worked really well.

00:35:03.660 --> 00:35:04.960
That's awesome.

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:09.810
I see Abigail, you have started
a thread here in the chat

00:35:09.810 --> 00:35:10.980
here too.

00:35:10.980 --> 00:35:14.010
If you want to jump in and
share any perspective there.

00:35:19.375 --> 00:35:20.000
And no worries.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:24.240
I don't want to put
anybody on the spot here.

00:35:24.240 --> 00:35:26.780
But yeah, any other
thoughts on this?

00:35:32.160 --> 00:35:36.130
I see, Ken, you had a
question around storytelling.

00:35:36.130 --> 00:35:38.975
Do you want to jump in
and ask that to everybody?

00:35:42.020 --> 00:35:42.520
Sure.

00:35:45.900 --> 00:35:47.370
Get myself on video here.

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:49.840
Yeah.

00:35:49.840 --> 00:35:51.880
I think the
discussion going back,

00:35:51.880 --> 00:35:56.530
I think Mr. Smith was the
one who kicked it off really

00:35:56.530 --> 00:36:02.410
of how do you get decision
makers to buy into your ideas?

00:36:02.410 --> 00:36:03.910
Some of the stuff I'm seeing--

00:36:03.910 --> 00:36:07.600
I do analyst
relations for Posit.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:09.880
So I'm talking with some of
the market research firms

00:36:09.880 --> 00:36:11.950
like Gartner and Forrester.

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:14.050
There's a lot of there's
a lot of research there

00:36:14.050 --> 00:36:15.940
that talks about the use of--

00:36:15.940 --> 00:36:20.590
the correct use of the right
tool to get your point across.

00:36:20.590 --> 00:36:24.850
And I wondered if any of you had
been taking data storytelling

00:36:24.850 --> 00:36:27.940
courses at any point?

00:36:27.940 --> 00:36:32.290
Some of you have replied
here to help you figure out

00:36:32.290 --> 00:36:34.810
what's the right tool to get
across how do you present data

00:36:34.810 --> 00:36:37.690
to be able to persuade people?

00:36:37.690 --> 00:36:40.400
And then another
point that came up

00:36:40.400 --> 00:36:44.830
I thought was also
interesting was

00:36:44.830 --> 00:36:49.050
the idea of there's
proactive storytelling

00:36:49.050 --> 00:36:54.030
versus using a self
service method of getting

00:36:54.030 --> 00:36:55.000
your ideas out there.

00:36:55.000 --> 00:36:57.060
So both of those
topics, I think, are--

00:36:57.060 --> 00:37:01.530
I find very, very useful.

00:37:01.530 --> 00:37:05.670
And I know that Rachel's found
some other customers that--

00:37:05.670 --> 00:37:10.980
one customer talked about
using an interactive web

00:37:10.980 --> 00:37:17.070
app versus sending out
a 200 page PDF report

00:37:17.070 --> 00:37:19.890
and how that really
changed their interactions

00:37:19.890 --> 00:37:21.190
with decision makers.

00:37:21.190 --> 00:37:25.440
So it's curious to hear
more about other people's

00:37:25.440 --> 00:37:30.050
experiences in that respect

00:37:30.050 --> 00:37:30.550
I know.

00:37:30.550 --> 00:37:34.390
There is a few people who had
thoughts on this in the chat.

00:37:34.390 --> 00:37:36.790
And Jim, I see you
were first there

00:37:36.790 --> 00:37:40.990
do you want to share
any perspective here?

00:37:40.990 --> 00:37:44.080
Just really quickly.

00:37:44.080 --> 00:37:51.230
I find that we end up going
down one of two paths often

00:37:51.230 --> 00:37:54.885
depending on the client
and the environment

00:37:54.885 --> 00:37:56.260
and the skill sets
of the people.

00:37:58.790 --> 00:38:02.720
Sometimes it is in fact
a matter of storytelling

00:38:02.720 --> 00:38:10.850
and getting it down into maybe
even a 5 minute set of slides.

00:38:10.850 --> 00:38:16.810
But just as often,
the exercise is

00:38:16.810 --> 00:38:19.780
a matter of empowering
other analysts

00:38:19.780 --> 00:38:22.840
or other operational
people whose day

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:28.690
job is doing whatever, and we
want to make their life better.

00:38:28.690 --> 00:38:30.420
And so we're
going to push data

00:38:30.420 --> 00:38:33.820
through some kind of
a layer to an endpoint

00:38:33.820 --> 00:38:36.580
so they can use it in the
system they're comfortable with.

00:38:36.580 --> 00:38:40.890
And so it's more of a using
the endpoint that they're

00:38:40.890 --> 00:38:44.700
comfortable with and
maybe less storytelling,

00:38:44.700 --> 00:38:51.420
more developing empathy for the
person you're trying to help

00:38:51.420 --> 00:38:52.650
do what they do better.

00:38:56.740 --> 00:38:57.700
Thank you.

00:38:57.700 --> 00:38:59.970
I see Marlene, you have
your hand raised too.

00:38:59.970 --> 00:39:01.200
Want to jump in?

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:02.490
Yeah.

00:39:02.490 --> 00:39:05.128
So I'm in a master's
program right now,

00:39:05.128 --> 00:39:07.170
and I'm literally in the
middle of a class that's

00:39:07.170 --> 00:39:10.500
focused completely around
communicating data, which

00:39:10.500 --> 00:39:11.940
I found admirable.

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:13.620
They actually spend the time--

00:39:13.620 --> 00:39:15.990
it's not even-- you're
not touching data

00:39:15.990 --> 00:39:17.470
at all in this class.

00:39:17.470 --> 00:39:20.790
They want you to make sure
that you identify your target

00:39:20.790 --> 00:39:24.810
audience, that you use the right
tone and language, that you

00:39:24.810 --> 00:39:27.450
make your point easy
to follow, things

00:39:27.450 --> 00:39:30.300
like that we take for granted
and are super nuanced,

00:39:30.300 --> 00:39:32.800
just like presentation giving.

00:39:32.800 --> 00:39:35.340
They force you to really
reexamine your habits,

00:39:35.340 --> 00:39:37.930
use concise language.

00:39:37.930 --> 00:39:42.120
And so I think that goes in
line with what the comments made

00:39:42.120 --> 00:39:44.010
about communicating data.

00:39:44.010 --> 00:39:46.140
It's not just an
effective visualization

00:39:46.140 --> 00:39:49.080
or an effective tool to
get the point across,

00:39:49.080 --> 00:39:53.490
but really making sure your
words back up your point.

00:39:53.490 --> 00:39:57.257
And then secondly, I went
to a water data conference

00:39:57.257 --> 00:39:58.590
out here in Southern California.

00:39:58.590 --> 00:40:00.257
I don't know if anybody
else on the call

00:40:00.257 --> 00:40:01.268
was able to go to this.

00:40:01.268 --> 00:40:03.060
It's called the California
Data Conference,

00:40:03.060 --> 00:40:04.200
but it's all around water.

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:06.010
So it's a little misleading.

00:40:06.010 --> 00:40:08.010
But there was an excellent
speaker, and her name

00:40:08.010 --> 00:40:09.420
and eludes me right now.

00:40:09.420 --> 00:40:11.880
But she gave me an
entire presentation

00:40:11.880 --> 00:40:13.350
that was just gorgeous.

00:40:13.350 --> 00:40:15.720
And it was about
highlighting signals so

00:40:15.720 --> 00:40:19.200
that when you're talking
to somebody in management,

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:22.110
that you're not just giving
them a bunch of data fluff.

00:40:22.110 --> 00:40:24.540
That you're really
getting their attention

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:30.930
to the part of the visual that
the outliers or the signal

00:40:30.930 --> 00:40:31.532
and whatnot.

00:40:31.532 --> 00:40:33.240
And she also referenced
a couple of books

00:40:33.240 --> 00:40:35.640
that I thought were incredible.

00:40:35.640 --> 00:40:37.470
One was-- sorry.

00:40:37.470 --> 00:40:38.460
It's called Signal.

00:40:38.460 --> 00:40:41.100
I believe it's out of
print now, but there

00:40:41.100 --> 00:40:42.760
is a newer version of it.

00:40:42.760 --> 00:40:45.210
So maybe you can hunt it down.

00:40:45.210 --> 00:40:49.620
But yes, I think totally,
it's about saying it well,

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:52.560
saying it short and concise,
saying the language they

00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:55.740
understand, and then giving
them the visual to back it

00:40:55.740 --> 00:40:56.967
up totally.

00:40:56.967 --> 00:40:58.800
Because usually if you
put it in their hands

00:40:58.800 --> 00:41:01.860
and they get some real
visceral feel around what

00:41:01.860 --> 00:41:03.468
the possibility is, it helps.

00:41:03.468 --> 00:41:05.760
But half the time, you can't
get in the door with them,

00:41:05.760 --> 00:41:08.260
and that's where probably the
other half of us who are still

00:41:08.260 --> 00:41:11.280
in that frustrated
vicious cycle of not being

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:15.300
able to get management to help
really build infrastructure

00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:19.470
or tools or teams to support
your work are falling into.

00:41:19.470 --> 00:41:20.388
So--

00:41:20.388 --> 00:41:21.930
It's interesting
when you said it's

00:41:21.930 --> 00:41:24.030
hard to get in the door
with decision makers.

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:28.660
What makes it hard?

00:41:28.660 --> 00:41:33.060
So some of it is time
because there are usually

00:41:33.060 --> 00:41:36.060
1,000 other things on
their plate that will be--

00:41:36.060 --> 00:41:39.780
take more precedent than we
need our infrastructure updated.

00:41:39.780 --> 00:41:41.470
Unless you're
building is on fire,

00:41:41.470 --> 00:41:43.860
they're never going to
see that as something that

00:41:43.860 --> 00:41:45.270
really needs attention.

00:41:45.270 --> 00:41:48.540
At least that's what my
organization has been like.

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:50.700
They're like, our
computers still run.

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:51.990
We're still crunching data.

00:41:51.990 --> 00:41:54.120
Our analysts still
do these reports.

00:41:54.120 --> 00:41:55.510
We're good.

00:41:55.510 --> 00:41:57.990
We don't need a single
source of truth or anything

00:41:57.990 --> 00:42:00.930
like that, which is crazy.

00:42:00.930 --> 00:42:03.840
And then the other part is,
even if you get in the door,

00:42:03.840 --> 00:42:07.080
it's, well, what
is the ROI on this?

00:42:07.080 --> 00:42:08.820
And sometimes it's hard to do.

00:42:08.820 --> 00:42:10.560
I don't have people's salaries.

00:42:10.560 --> 00:42:13.262
I can go to HR or
to our accounting

00:42:13.262 --> 00:42:14.970
and try to get some
of those projections,

00:42:14.970 --> 00:42:17.610
but it also seems
off putting to them

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:21.330
that this analyst scientists
in the corner of the company

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:22.860
is asking for these things.

00:42:22.860 --> 00:42:24.870
So it just-- it can
be tricky to try

00:42:24.870 --> 00:42:28.440
to give the value add right
up front where they go,

00:42:28.440 --> 00:42:29.760
aha, I get it.

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:32.190
Instead, you have to
say, something's broken

00:42:32.190 --> 00:42:33.752
and I need to fix it.

00:42:33.752 --> 00:42:36.210
And then you have to prove that
it's actually broken enough

00:42:36.210 --> 00:42:37.320
that it needs fixing.

00:42:37.320 --> 00:42:41.340
So I hope that makes sense, but
those are kind of the two cases

00:42:41.340 --> 00:42:43.770
I've run into, both in
the same organization.

00:42:46.555 --> 00:42:47.430
Thank you, Marlene.

00:42:47.430 --> 00:42:49.320
That course sounds great too.

00:42:49.320 --> 00:42:51.180
You're reminding me that I--

00:42:51.180 --> 00:42:52.920
oh, wow, that link
is really long.

00:42:52.920 --> 00:42:56.610
But you're reminding me, I just
bought this everyday business

00:42:56.610 --> 00:42:59.430
storytelling book and I
forgot to actually read it

00:42:59.430 --> 00:43:00.810
when it arrived.

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:04.500
But one of the lessons
learned I saw--

00:43:04.500 --> 00:43:08.770
and it was in your PowerPoints,
like your headers--

00:43:08.770 --> 00:43:10.020
or do you have it right there?

00:43:10.020 --> 00:43:12.300
Oh, I see you grabbing a book.

00:43:12.300 --> 00:43:15.540
Instead of putting like
Q3 results or something

00:43:15.540 --> 00:43:17.160
that really doesn't
mean anything

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:19.410
at all at the top
of your PowerPoint,

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:24.120
actually putting the value there
and what you're trying to show.

00:43:24.120 --> 00:43:26.898
Alan, I see you had some
thoughts on this too.

00:43:26.898 --> 00:43:28.065
Do you want to jump in next?

00:43:33.650 --> 00:43:38.030
And if you change your mind
and want to jump in after,

00:43:38.030 --> 00:43:38.630
let me know.

00:43:38.630 --> 00:43:42.920
Anybody else have any thoughts
on the data storytelling piece.

00:43:42.920 --> 00:43:44.840
I think Adam, you
were sharing something

00:43:44.840 --> 00:43:48.030
about a course you took?

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:48.530
Yeah.

00:43:48.530 --> 00:43:51.110
If you've ever read the
storytelling with data book.

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:53.450
It's super great way
to think about how

00:43:53.450 --> 00:43:56.730
to distill a draft down there.

00:43:56.730 --> 00:43:57.230
Yeah.

00:43:57.230 --> 00:44:00.350
It's like, how do you distill
the graph down to its essence.

00:44:00.350 --> 00:44:03.350
And the workshop I took with
them was really interesting

00:44:03.350 --> 00:44:06.980
because they really
focus hard on every graph

00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:09.350
should just have one big idea.

00:44:09.350 --> 00:44:12.800
And if you're not telling one
single individual isolated

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:15.770
story with that graphic,
it's going to confuse people.

00:44:15.770 --> 00:44:18.170
And so you should just
keep working on refining

00:44:18.170 --> 00:44:20.960
that until it's just that
one story and then make

00:44:20.960 --> 00:44:23.630
it super obvious
what you're actually

00:44:23.630 --> 00:44:28.280
trying to say with a title that
says, sales decreased whatever,

00:44:28.280 --> 00:44:30.030
last quarter.

00:44:30.030 --> 00:44:31.160
That's the whole story.

00:44:31.160 --> 00:44:34.400
So just being super
clear and making sure

00:44:34.400 --> 00:44:38.270
that each slide, each exhibit
is just one specific thing you

00:44:38.270 --> 00:44:40.890
want to get across because
people are so bad at juggling

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:42.890
what they've learned so
far in your presentation

00:44:42.890 --> 00:44:46.002
and what they're looking at now
and then thinking about what

00:44:46.002 --> 00:44:47.210
they're going have for lunch.

00:44:47.210 --> 00:44:48.793
And so making it as
simple as possible

00:44:48.793 --> 00:44:50.690
to get that message across.

00:44:50.690 --> 00:44:51.440
Definitely.

00:44:51.440 --> 00:44:55.160
That makes me think about the
conversation about presenting

00:44:55.160 --> 00:44:57.710
to our executives as well.

00:44:57.710 --> 00:45:01.500
Having one thing in mind when
you are meeting with them,

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:02.390
have a tendency.

00:45:02.390 --> 00:45:05.450
Because you don't get
somebody's time all the--

00:45:05.450 --> 00:45:07.730
as often as you want,
you want to tell them

00:45:07.730 --> 00:45:11.180
everything in that 30
minute meeting instead

00:45:11.180 --> 00:45:12.590
of focusing on one.

00:45:12.590 --> 00:45:13.800
Mike, I see your hand up.

00:45:13.800 --> 00:45:14.300
Yeah.

00:45:14.300 --> 00:45:18.920
This is just following on
from what Adams just said.

00:45:18.920 --> 00:45:20.420
Yes, absolutely.

00:45:20.420 --> 00:45:22.400
I would love if that
was the way that we

00:45:22.400 --> 00:45:24.440
presented data and graphs.

00:45:24.440 --> 00:45:29.040
But there's also the
tension that says, well,

00:45:29.040 --> 00:45:33.020
if anyone takes this
graph out of context copy

00:45:33.020 --> 00:45:35.180
paste it and it winds
up in front of the CEO

00:45:35.180 --> 00:45:38.150
and none of the context is there
because I'm not presenting it,

00:45:38.150 --> 00:45:40.550
then I must cram
every single hedging

00:45:40.550 --> 00:45:42.270
statement onto this graph.

00:45:42.270 --> 00:45:44.575
And so the actual
useful stuff becomes

00:45:44.575 --> 00:45:45.950
like the litter
box in the middle

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:49.980
with all of that extra fluff.

00:45:49.980 --> 00:45:54.950
I so wish that we could have it
so that your graph isn't just

00:45:54.950 --> 00:45:58.100
a static thing, but
it has hover overs

00:45:58.100 --> 00:46:00.350
or whatever else
you need to convey

00:46:00.350 --> 00:46:04.110
that additional
contextual information.

00:46:04.110 --> 00:46:06.560
But I think the
danger is that people

00:46:06.560 --> 00:46:10.295
try and have a graph that
meets the needs of 15

00:46:10.295 --> 00:46:11.045
different parties.

00:46:13.952 --> 00:46:15.410
First slides that
have a button--

00:46:15.410 --> 00:46:16.840
what's your business function?

00:46:16.840 --> 00:46:19.340
Click that and then it's going
to fill everything in for you

00:46:19.340 --> 00:46:20.298
based on that function.

00:46:20.298 --> 00:46:22.880
And it might say,
no, you can't see this.

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.490
That's right.

00:46:25.490 --> 00:46:28.280
Or best call me,
it's complicated.

00:46:31.370 --> 00:46:34.280
Well actually, do you
always put your like contact

00:46:34.280 --> 00:46:40.650
information on graphs
and slides you put out?

00:46:40.650 --> 00:46:44.020
Does anybody do that?

00:46:44.020 --> 00:46:45.030
No.

00:46:45.030 --> 00:46:45.660
Oh, yeah

00:46:45.660 --> 00:46:46.960
[INAUDIBLE] the idea though.

00:46:46.960 --> 00:46:47.460
Yeah--

00:46:47.460 --> 00:46:49.192
It's complicated.

00:46:49.192 --> 00:46:51.400
I worked for an organization
that did always have--

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:54.310
every dashboard and
thing had a thing.

00:46:54.310 --> 00:46:58.600
Somebody to contact within
the organization that made it

00:46:58.600 --> 00:47:01.610
or who owns the data behind it.

00:47:01.610 --> 00:47:04.820
I started doing that
on random Google Docs

00:47:04.820 --> 00:47:07.160
that I share across the
company, making sure

00:47:07.160 --> 00:47:09.270
that my name is
at the top of it.

00:47:09.270 --> 00:47:11.210
So if anybody has
questions, they

00:47:11.210 --> 00:47:16.200
know who originally made
this and to reach out to me.

00:47:16.200 --> 00:47:18.350
I had a leader
a couple of years

00:47:18.350 --> 00:47:22.460
ago ask if we could put
QR codes next to the plots

00:47:22.460 --> 00:47:24.897
that we put in the slide
as a way for people

00:47:24.897 --> 00:47:26.480
to get in touch with
more information.

00:47:26.480 --> 00:47:28.980
And sure enough, there's an R
package that can make QR code.

00:47:28.980 --> 00:47:30.260
So there you go.

00:47:33.280 --> 00:47:36.910
I see somebody had asked a
question anonymously that was,

00:47:36.910 --> 00:47:39.490
no matter how much I
practice, they always panic

00:47:39.490 --> 00:47:41.080
when I have a presentation.

00:47:41.080 --> 00:47:43.360
Does anybody have tips
you'd like to share

00:47:43.360 --> 00:47:46.220
regarding public speaking?

00:47:46.220 --> 00:47:46.720
Yes.

00:47:46.720 --> 00:47:48.820
Have a quick
one, do a podcast.

00:47:48.820 --> 00:47:50.020
I'm kidding, I'm kidding.

00:47:50.020 --> 00:47:53.650
It actually did help
me, though honestly, so.

00:47:53.650 --> 00:47:58.570
So it would be unnatural
not to feel nervous.

00:47:58.570 --> 00:47:59.740
Everyone feels nervous.

00:47:59.740 --> 00:48:01.660
Even the people that don't
look as though they're nervous

00:48:01.660 --> 00:48:02.228
are nervous.

00:48:02.228 --> 00:48:03.895
It's just they're
managing those nerves.

00:48:06.700 --> 00:48:11.440
The other thing I tend to
think of is, talk to people

00:48:11.440 --> 00:48:15.610
as though you're just having
a conversation with friends.

00:48:15.610 --> 00:48:19.900
Don't use language that is more
complex than you would normally

00:48:19.900 --> 00:48:23.200
use or--

00:48:23.200 --> 00:48:26.320
I know some people work well
with they can put on a persona

00:48:26.320 --> 00:48:29.200
or put on a hat in
order to present.

00:48:29.200 --> 00:48:32.650
But honestly, I tend
to just picking out

00:48:32.650 --> 00:48:34.120
one or two people
in the audience

00:48:34.120 --> 00:48:36.695
and just talk to them like
I was having a conversation.

00:48:39.370 --> 00:48:42.610
Kaitlin, I see you
have your hand raised.

00:48:42.610 --> 00:48:45.257
Yeah, Mike, those were
really great points.

00:48:45.257 --> 00:48:46.590
Feel like I can talk about this.

00:48:46.590 --> 00:48:51.120
I just finished a one hour
workshop at a conference.

00:48:51.120 --> 00:48:54.750
So I think the biggest
tip-- and yeah,

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:56.227
you hit the nail on the head.

00:48:56.227 --> 00:48:57.810
Of course, you're
going to be nervous.

00:48:57.810 --> 00:49:00.870
Like 30 minutes before, my hands
were sweating and I was like,

00:49:00.870 --> 00:49:04.110
oh gosh, am I going
to completely blank.

00:49:04.110 --> 00:49:06.630
But no, I think once you--

00:49:06.630 --> 00:49:10.775
so something I do is
I count myself down

00:49:10.775 --> 00:49:12.900
because really, it only
takes one second of courage

00:49:12.900 --> 00:49:16.030
because once you're up there,
you can't really go back.

00:49:16.030 --> 00:49:21.630
So it's in my head, I'm 3,
2, 1, go, and then you go.

00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:26.370
And I think during
the start of my,

00:49:26.370 --> 00:49:29.040
I guess, public speaking
engagements or career,

00:49:29.040 --> 00:49:33.210
I did put on a
persona and stuff.

00:49:33.210 --> 00:49:38.040
But now that I've
done it more and more,

00:49:38.040 --> 00:49:43.680
it's your best bet to just
be yourself and talk to the--

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:45.480
and I think it's so
important to remember,

00:49:45.480 --> 00:49:49.050
I feel like public speaking
it's so easy to just focus on

00:49:49.050 --> 00:49:52.420
yourself and how
you may be perceived

00:49:52.420 --> 00:49:53.670
by the audience this and that.

00:49:53.670 --> 00:49:55.980
But most of the
time, the audience

00:49:55.980 --> 00:49:57.670
is just there to learn.

00:49:57.670 --> 00:50:00.540
So in whatever way
you can provide value

00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:03.040
without just being so in your
head about what you look like,

00:50:03.040 --> 00:50:06.640
what you sound like, what
content you're even presenting,

00:50:06.640 --> 00:50:08.640
yeah, I think it's so
important just to remember

00:50:08.640 --> 00:50:09.723
why the audience is there.

00:50:09.723 --> 00:50:11.910
And they're there to learn.

00:50:11.910 --> 00:50:16.590
And then the one last thing that
I can say about public speaking

00:50:16.590 --> 00:50:19.320
that's really helped me is if
you are doing a public speaking

00:50:19.320 --> 00:50:23.280
engagement, do
something on a topic

00:50:23.280 --> 00:50:25.350
that you feel very
comfortable talking about.

00:50:25.350 --> 00:50:26.725
That you could
talk on this topic

00:50:26.725 --> 00:50:27.990
for hours and hours and hours.

00:50:27.990 --> 00:50:30.750
Because I've done presentations
where I didn't really feel

00:50:30.750 --> 00:50:33.225
super confident about the topic.

00:50:33.225 --> 00:50:36.120
Once I did a workshop
on data visualizations.

00:50:36.120 --> 00:50:41.220
And although I had spent a
whole year not making a data

00:50:41.220 --> 00:50:45.540
visualization at all, I was just
doing data analysis pipelines

00:50:45.540 --> 00:50:49.038
in R. So that public
speaking engagement, I was--

00:50:49.038 --> 00:50:50.580
I remember I was so
nervous because I

00:50:50.580 --> 00:50:53.190
didn't feel so confident on
the topic I was presenting.

00:50:53.190 --> 00:50:57.280
So yeah, just make sure
your topic really well.

00:50:57.280 --> 00:51:02.880
And then I think from there,
it can flow really natural.

00:51:02.880 --> 00:51:04.795
Those are all great points.

00:51:04.795 --> 00:51:06.420
What you were saying,
Caitlin, was just

00:51:06.420 --> 00:51:10.290
making me think about,
sometimes what has been helpful

00:51:10.290 --> 00:51:12.810
for me is to reach out to
a few people who I know

00:51:12.810 --> 00:51:15.700
are going to be
there ahead of time,

00:51:15.700 --> 00:51:19.920
and just asking them maybe
questions about what they would

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:21.687
like to learn or--

00:51:21.687 --> 00:51:23.520
I don't know, just
reaching out to them in--

00:51:23.580 --> 00:51:24.900
--general to talk to them.

00:51:24.900 --> 00:51:29.220
Because then at least part of
what I am providing or sharing

00:51:29.220 --> 00:51:32.220
is providing value to
somebody in the audience,

00:51:32.220 --> 00:51:34.680
and it makes me a
lot less nervous.

00:51:34.680 --> 00:51:38.040
I guess also helps with
any imposter syndrome too

00:51:38.040 --> 00:51:41.010
to know that this is going
to be valuable for at least

00:51:41.010 --> 00:51:41.970
these people.

00:51:41.970 --> 00:51:45.210
So chances are, it will be
valuable for their other peers

00:51:45.210 --> 00:51:46.720
that are too.

00:51:46.720 --> 00:51:47.220
Yeah.

00:51:47.220 --> 00:51:47.910
No, for sure.

00:51:47.910 --> 00:51:50.937
And even yesterday,
I was talking--

00:51:50.937 --> 00:51:52.770
so I presented today
and then the conference

00:51:52.770 --> 00:51:53.770
was yesterday and today.

00:51:53.770 --> 00:51:56.550
And I was talking to people
who registered for my workshop

00:51:56.550 --> 00:51:58.290
and that made me
feel so much better.

00:51:58.290 --> 00:52:00.480
And even seeing them
today in the audience,

00:52:00.480 --> 00:52:03.510
those were the ones that I
was looking at and talking to.

00:52:03.510 --> 00:52:05.790
You know what I mean?

00:52:05.790 --> 00:52:08.430
Absolutely I was just
talking with my coworker who's

00:52:08.430 --> 00:52:10.920
going to a Pi data
conference about this too.

00:52:10.920 --> 00:52:16.230
And I was just saying, if we do
have a list of who is attending

00:52:16.230 --> 00:52:19.290
and if you can reach out to
somebody on LinkedIn ahead

00:52:19.290 --> 00:52:21.900
of time or just
introduce yourself,

00:52:21.900 --> 00:52:25.780
it makes you feel so much more
comfortable when you're there,

00:52:25.780 --> 00:52:27.600
that you at least know somebody.

00:52:30.230 --> 00:52:32.465
Any other thoughts or tips?

00:52:36.450 --> 00:52:40.322
I really liked Ahnas' tip
about dressing comfortably.

00:52:40.322 --> 00:52:42.405
Dressing something that
you're comfortable wearing

00:52:42.405 --> 00:52:44.580
and that you're not going
to be constantly thinking

00:52:44.580 --> 00:52:48.970
about what you're wearing,
that's super important.

00:52:48.970 --> 00:52:49.560
Absolutely.

00:52:49.560 --> 00:52:52.050
I've noticed that if I
have a presentation to do,

00:52:52.050 --> 00:52:54.215
I'll pick an outfit that
makes me very comfortable.

00:52:54.215 --> 00:52:56.340
I'll even get a haircut or
I'll do something like--

00:52:56.340 --> 00:52:56.910
Yes.

00:52:56.910 --> 00:52:59.520
Those are little things
that will definitely

00:52:59.520 --> 00:53:03.730
make you feel better.

00:53:03.730 --> 00:53:04.330
Yeah.

00:53:04.330 --> 00:53:06.100
Minimize all the other stuff.

00:53:06.100 --> 00:53:08.290
The peripheral stuff that
could make you nervous,

00:53:08.290 --> 00:53:10.450
that's not you
speaking because you

00:53:10.450 --> 00:53:12.050
don't need that in your life.

00:53:12.050 --> 00:53:13.300
You're already nervous enough.

00:53:15.940 --> 00:53:16.560
Absolutely.

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:18.570
I see a few people talking
about Toastmasters.

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:23.320
Has anybody been
part of Toastmasters?

00:53:23.320 --> 00:53:24.870
I'm seeing some nods.

00:53:24.870 --> 00:53:27.532
I've never been.

00:53:27.532 --> 00:53:28.990
A lot of places
will let you drop

00:53:28.990 --> 00:53:33.190
in if you want to go to a group
and say, hey, I'm interested

00:53:33.190 --> 00:53:35.990
but I'm not sure if
I want to do this.

00:53:35.990 --> 00:53:39.300
A lot of them are really happy
to have you spectate and clap

00:53:39.300 --> 00:53:41.300
for people when they
finish and stuff like that.

00:53:41.300 --> 00:53:42.970
So I recommend anybody
who would check it out.

00:53:42.970 --> 00:53:44.440
But it's usually
not data focused.

00:53:44.440 --> 00:53:47.380
There's a good topic
each time, each session.

00:53:47.380 --> 00:53:49.980
It's not data, but helpful.

00:53:49.980 --> 00:53:50.480
Cool.

00:53:50.480 --> 00:53:53.080
I've done super technical
data talks to them,

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:55.205
and it's helpful because
talking to an audience who

00:53:55.205 --> 00:53:58.670
has no idea what your
domain knowledge is

00:53:58.670 --> 00:54:00.380
can make you like, oh, OK.

00:54:00.380 --> 00:54:03.380
I have to make sure this
jargon is to a minimum,

00:54:03.380 --> 00:54:06.290
and I have to explain this
like I'm explaining it

00:54:06.290 --> 00:54:09.900
to a really smart junior high
student instead of my peers.

00:54:09.900 --> 00:54:12.240
So it can be really helpful.

00:54:12.240 --> 00:54:14.750
They also have-- a
meeting as an hour,

00:54:14.750 --> 00:54:19.760
and they will have three
scheduled big presentations,

00:54:19.760 --> 00:54:22.550
like 15, 20 minutes, but
they also have little drills.

00:54:22.550 --> 00:54:24.890
And even as a first
timer, you can go and try

00:54:24.890 --> 00:54:28.320
one of the little drills where
you get up for 30 seconds

00:54:28.320 --> 00:54:30.020
and they ask you
something, and you just

00:54:30.020 --> 00:54:32.090
have to speak off the
cuff and it works out

00:54:32.090 --> 00:54:33.500
some of those jitters.

00:54:33.500 --> 00:54:36.020
Even if you're introverted,
even if you're scared,

00:54:36.020 --> 00:54:38.120
it's worth doing because
if it doesn't scare you,

00:54:38.120 --> 00:54:39.485
won't change you.

00:54:39.485 --> 00:54:40.610
Totally worth doing though.

00:54:40.610 --> 00:54:42.193
Everybody should go
to one Toastmaster

00:54:42.193 --> 00:54:44.510
meeting in their life, I'd say.

00:54:44.510 --> 00:54:46.050
Cool.

00:54:46.050 --> 00:54:50.750
We had for the POSIT conference,
all the accepted speakers

00:54:50.750 --> 00:54:54.020
get speaker training
through articulation.

00:54:54.020 --> 00:54:56.660
So I got to go through
that last year.

00:54:56.660 --> 00:55:00.680
And it was really helpful
to think about starting off

00:55:00.680 --> 00:55:02.570
your talk with a story.

00:55:02.570 --> 00:55:06.890
Framing things as a story
to bring the audience in.

00:55:06.890 --> 00:55:09.770
And I've been starting to try
and do that with presentations

00:55:09.770 --> 00:55:11.830
that I give internally as well.

00:55:11.830 --> 00:55:15.440
Like who is the customer
that this is impacting

00:55:15.440 --> 00:55:19.670
and why are we-- why am
I presenting this too?

00:55:19.670 --> 00:55:21.975
Jared, I see you had asked
a question in the chat

00:55:21.975 --> 00:55:22.850
a little bit earlier.

00:55:22.850 --> 00:55:24.740
I'm sorry we didn't
get to it until now.

00:55:24.740 --> 00:55:25.730
Do you want to jump in?

00:55:29.550 --> 00:55:31.920
And Jared, I'll read--
oh, I can see you unmuted.

00:55:31.920 --> 00:55:32.590
There you go.

00:55:32.590 --> 00:55:33.090
Am I now?

00:55:33.090 --> 00:55:33.930
Can you guys hear?

00:55:33.930 --> 00:55:34.890
Yes.

00:55:34.890 --> 00:55:35.910
Sure.

00:55:35.910 --> 00:55:38.400
This is just more open ended
for anybody's experience

00:55:38.400 --> 00:55:42.510
when it comes to giving
presentations or--

00:55:42.510 --> 00:55:44.610
I mean, I know it has more
to do with the audience

00:55:44.610 --> 00:55:49.980
that you're in front of, but
trying to avoid the sales pitch

00:55:49.980 --> 00:55:53.580
type of presentation versus
keeping things much more

00:55:53.580 --> 00:55:59.052
unbiased and just presenting.

00:55:59.052 --> 00:56:01.260
Giving an unbiased approach
to what you're presenting

00:56:01.260 --> 00:56:03.540
instead of trying to push
an agenda essentially.

00:56:03.540 --> 00:56:05.670
Because I've gone
both ways where

00:56:05.670 --> 00:56:10.440
if I'm trying to essentially
persuade decision

00:56:10.440 --> 00:56:13.980
makers on my idea
or pitch my idea,

00:56:13.980 --> 00:56:16.650
then it obviously
becomes straight

00:56:16.650 --> 00:56:18.940
into a sales pitch scenario.

00:56:18.940 --> 00:56:23.580
But I've also tried to
just do presentations

00:56:23.580 --> 00:56:28.500
on giving the information in
a very unbiased disseminating

00:56:28.500 --> 00:56:31.290
the information to the public
audience type scenario.

00:56:31.290 --> 00:56:34.080
And I always run
the gamut gambit

00:56:34.080 --> 00:56:41.820
of how to not include your own
personal bias when presenting

00:56:41.820 --> 00:56:45.630
and try to keep things very
open, if that makes sense.

00:56:45.630 --> 00:56:47.505
Just wanted to get
everybody's opinion.

00:56:52.130 --> 00:56:55.010
Anybody have thoughts
to share there?

00:56:57.818 --> 00:56:59.770
Kind of want Jim
to explain himself.

00:57:04.080 --> 00:57:09.360
Again, we're tool people and
maybe even statisticians here.

00:57:09.360 --> 00:57:14.740
What we bring in terms of our
backgrounds and skills matters.

00:57:14.740 --> 00:57:23.140
And so more often than
not, the opening slide

00:57:23.140 --> 00:57:25.630
is going to say who
we are and process

00:57:25.630 --> 00:57:29.690
we may be used to arrive at
this piece of information,

00:57:29.690 --> 00:57:33.520
and that's a pitch.

00:57:33.520 --> 00:57:37.470
Now, bias may be
implies that we've

00:57:37.470 --> 00:57:40.800
imprinted something unnatural
or a distortion on this,

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:44.752
and I don't mean to
misconstrue what you--

00:57:44.752 --> 00:57:45.960
construe what you said there.

00:57:45.960 --> 00:57:49.980
But I don't undersell
the skills we

00:57:49.980 --> 00:57:52.950
bring as people to
the conversations

00:57:52.950 --> 00:57:58.230
with our organizations, whether
it's a government institution

00:57:58.230 --> 00:58:00.480
or a charity or a business.

00:58:00.480 --> 00:58:05.660
We bring something that's
valuable and hugely valuable,

00:58:05.660 --> 00:58:06.160
in fact.

00:58:06.160 --> 00:58:11.020
So we shouldn't be ashamed
to imprint that on the work

00:58:11.020 --> 00:58:15.040
we've done, and the
results that we've arrived.

00:58:15.040 --> 00:58:18.100
At least that's where
I'm coming from.

00:58:21.660 --> 00:58:22.620
Great points, Jim.

00:58:22.620 --> 00:58:24.740
Thank you.

00:58:24.740 --> 00:58:30.380
Well, thank you all so much for
joining today and winging it

00:58:30.380 --> 00:58:31.530
with us.

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:32.760
This has been really fun.

00:58:32.760 --> 00:58:35.630
I appreciate everybody
jumping in here

00:58:35.630 --> 00:58:38.950
and being the
featured leader too.

